#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5756ce-4709-5ad0-8ac0-336e04a07147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351553} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d94ba1-f6fb-565d-aa03-917dacfde257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351554} {:answer/id #uuid "23a222e3-0df3-59bd-845f-536818990655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351555} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fadcc5-30ad-58f2-ae98-6868b696e959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351556} {:answer/id #uuid "055872c1-865f-5c07-a212-c3458a16c6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a5756ce-4709-5ad0-8ac0-336e04a07147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351553} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a723885-d517-5f98-9d23-ade68e8c13a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c80544f-2eae-5edd-8bb7-5e25399a9d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-128124" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70dc487f-b422-5eda-bf9a-41933ac786a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351560} {:answer/id #uuid "6347f32d-ea1a-511a-ac3d-398ea00fac6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351561} {:answer/id #uuid "87b920c0-c2c6-530f-9eeb-0037c3cb2c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351562} {:answer/id #uuid "6815e99b-39e5-5390-a7ff-a18b9ef9d82e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351563} {:answer/id #uuid "81c855a2-cc86-5577-9b7e-1ccc7c278a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87b920c0-c2c6-530f-9eeb-0037c3cb2c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351562} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfc6be7-d43c-53f2-8187-a2e87081d2d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3d3c32b-5e5b-571b-a2d8-3ef8f60e3dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-128151" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}$ does not exist\n- The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n- $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III gives us a true statement:  \n\n*If the system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then, $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a53765dd-7014-5b39-bf54-e8f3976b2384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113409} {:answer/id #uuid "13322188-8978-5455-b6c9-479a518c6596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113410} {:answer/id #uuid "22096abe-1943-50f3-99f0-aa1dafa8ad8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113411} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca7fc4f-bcc4-5cd1-80f3-0af1032a7077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113412} {:answer/id #uuid "55d76481-214e-51c0-a915-5a564235ade6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13322188-8978-5455-b6c9-479a518c6596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113410} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d8db78-0edf-543a-9a4c-fbbba37ddad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e42babae-3f60-5ad6-9c62-eaf91af5f106" :question/math-academy-id "q-32167" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and III give us true statements:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $det(A)\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "927442ac-f074-5663-bb7b-dce75fa1168a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114142} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bd71bb-aa34-54ae-8065-05453e14c1ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114143} {:answer/id #uuid "299bcd06-8c67-5d3a-a487-061b6a53ad67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114144} {:answer/id #uuid "2a88a714-4611-5599-b23f-8353ba101c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114145} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd2cee1-2aef-5655-83e9-a79a15ce692d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fd2cee1-2aef-5655-83e9-a79a15ce692d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114146} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff02f0ad-cfde-5b7e-aae8-4c949a7079ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07b54935-f6c1-5cc5-a220-23bab58f39a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-47653" :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$ intersect at infinitely many points" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot k-2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6k+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $6k+6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e2ddf0-f804-5a30-980d-66304828d094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351567} {:answer/id #uuid "5be870e4-e9bd-5937-987a-d79308c5cc3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351568} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2b59cd-65d9-5d5c-8608-f9aa1b253dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ are parallel" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351569} {:answer/id #uuid "81b9a644-f5fb-5579-a1e9-3121dea65ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution for the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351570} {:answer/id #uuid "bca0fdcf-aa0b-5275-8400-08e14037c3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca0fdcf-aa0b-5275-8400-08e14037c3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351571} :answer-field/id #uuid "266ea60b-4a4f-549a-9d0f-158bc22cdf50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b78864d-df1c-54a9-a660-cac490a85eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73608" :question/problem "Consider the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$"}]}
